cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite the accompanying paper."
title: "Measuring Pairwise Diversity with Linearly Many Comparisons"
authors:
  - family-names: Ray
    given-names: Shawn
    affiliation: "Carnegie Mellon University"
    email: shawnray@cmu.edu
repository-code: "https://github.com/shawnray-research/Graph-SND"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  Diversity is often measured as the average pairwise dissimilarity of n
  agents, models, or generated outputs. Exact evaluation needs quadratically
  many comparisons, and its single number hides how diversity is organized. We
  use the classical decomposition, or anatomy, of a pairwise table into a
  level, individual distinctiveness, and relational structure such as roles.
  The error of any subset-of-pairs average is exactly a distinctiveness term
  plus a relational term, and the first vanishes for every population if and
  only if each individual is compared equally often. With random labels, such
  balanced designs are unbiased, with closed-form variance set by relational
  structure alone. For every metric, m such comparisons have relative root-
  mean-square error at most m^(-1/2), so O(n + ε^-2) comparisons fixed in
  advance give relative error ε with constant probability, an optimal rate
  previously reached, to our knowledge, only adaptively; uniform pair sampling
  can need about n/2 times more. On trained multi-agent teams, classifier
  populations, and judge-scored language-model outputs, balanced designs need
  up to seven times fewer comparisons than uniform pairs; with a frontier-
  model judge, 128 of 2,016 calls estimate the diversity of 64 responses with
  1–2% root-mean-square error. The anatomy also separates two multi-agent
  tasks by how their diversity develops.
keywords:
  - diversity
  - incomplete U-statistics
  - experimental design
  - multi-agent reinforcement learning
  - evaluation of generative models
